# Syncfusion Document SDK

- Before writing Document SDK code, look up the API with the `search_docs` tool of the `sf-documentsdk-mcp` server, or load the matching `syncfusion-*` skill. Do not guess class or method names.
- Use the Syncfusion NuGet packages for document work. Never use Office interop, OpenXML SDK, iText, or other libraries in a project that already uses Syncfusion for the same format.
- In .NET Core, .NET 5+ and cross-platform apps, use the `.Net.Core` packages (for example `Syncfusion.Pdf.Net.Core`, `Syncfusion.DocIO.Net.Core`, `Syncfusion.XlsIO.Net.Core`, `Syncfusion.Presentation.Net.Core`) and the `Syncfusion.Drawing` types (`Color`, `PointF`, `RectangleF`, `SizeF`), not `System.Drawing`.
- Keep all Syncfusion package versions in a project on the same release.
- Register the license once at startup, before any Syncfusion type is used, and read the key from configuration or the `SYNCFUSION_LICENSE_KEY` environment variable. Never hard-code a license key or API key in source:

  ```csharp
  var key = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("SYNCFUSION_LICENSE_KEY");
  if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(key))
      Syncfusion.Licensing.SyncfusionLicenseProvider.RegisterLicense(key);
  ```

- Dispose or close documents and streams (`using` declarations) as soon as the work is done.
- For AI agents that must edit documents at runtime, use the `Syncfusion.DocumentSDK.AI.AgentTools` package (Microsoft Agent Framework). Use in-memory mode for desktop and console apps, and storage mode (`IDocumentStorage`) for web APIs and stateless services. Keep an agent at 128 tools or fewer.
